{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfUUBvvrPySQ2PZq8yWYb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Running the numbers on current volume:\n\n- West-coast orders: 35% × 4,500/wk ≈ 1,575/wk ≈ 81,900/year\n- **3PL cost**: $3.10 × 81,900 ≈ **$254k/year**\n- **Warehouse cost**: $420k lease + 8 hires. Even at a modest $50k fully-loaded cost per hire, that's $400k in labor alone → **~$820k/year**, before racking, WMS software, insurance, or packaging supplies — none of which are in your stated numbers.\n\nThat's a >3x cost gap in the 3PL's favor *today*. Outbound shipping cost should be roughly the same either way, so it nets out of the comparison.\n\n**Break-even volume**: solving $800k fixed cost = $3.10 × N gives N ≈ 258,000 orders/year (≈4,960/wk) — more than your *entire company's* current weekly volume, just for the west coast alone. Unless west-coast demand is on track to roughly triple in the next year or two, the warehouse won't pay for itself inside a 3-year lease.\n\n**Recommendation**: go 3PL now. Revisit the warehouse decision when west-coast volume approaches that ~5,000/wk range, or if packaging/returns quality from the 3PL demonstrably hurts retention/margin in a way you can quantify against the ~$566k/year premium. I'd skip splitting volume for now — running two fulfillment paths adds real operational complexity (two packaging experiences, two systems) that isn't justified until you're near the break-even point, at which point a split becomes a natural transition step rather than a permanent structure.\n\nThe biggest lever in this math is the hire cost assumption ($50k) — if your actual fully-loaded cost per warehouse worker is different, the break-even point moves accordingly. Want me to rerun it with your real numbers, or dig into the \"control over packaging/returns\" side quantitatively (e.g., what return-rate delta would justify the premium)?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_s